# The Web UI is built on Windows but rebuilt on Linux in CI (frontend-dist-in-sync
# diffs the committed bundle byte-for-byte). Force LF everywhere in the frontend
# and the committed bundle so a Windows checkout never rewrites EOLs — otherwise
# the committed dist (CRLF) differs from a Linux rebuild (LF) and the gate fails.
frontend/** text=auto eol=lf
kernel/webui/dist/** text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
scripts/*.sh text eol=lf
# Binary build assets in the committed bundle (M951 self-hosted fonts): exempt
# from the text/eol normalization above so git never rewrites their bytes —
# otherwise the woff2 is corrupted and the Linux rebuild diff would never match.
kernel/webui/dist/**/*.woff2 binary
kernel/webui/dist/**/*.woff binary
kernel/webui/dist/**/*.ttf binary
# Built-in skill bundles are go:embed-ded into the binary (M852). Force LF so a
# Windows checkout doesn't embed CRLF — the shell setup script must run on Linux.
plugins/builtinskills/** text eol=lf

# Go source: force LF, same reasoning as the lines above. gofmt normalises to
# LF, so a CRLF-stored .go file is reported by `gofmt -l` on line endings alone
# — which makes a formatting gate meaningless and hides real findings. At the
# time this rule was added, 673 of the module's 2,378 .go files were stored with
# CRLF and 1,705 with LF.
#
# Adding this rule is the declaration of intent. The tree still needs ONE
# follow-up to actually take effect:
#
#     git add --renormalize .
#
# Until that lands, `gofmt -l` over the Go roots stays red on those 673 files.
# See the commented-out `fmt` target in the Makefile for the gate to re-add once
# the tree is normalised.
*.go text eol=lf

# The generated structure docs are rewritten by `make structure-md` on whatever
# platform the developer is on. Without this rule `core.autocrlf=true` turns
# them into a phantom diff ("LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git
# touches it") and `structure-md -check` churns on a clean tree.
.project/STRUCTURE.generated/** text eol=lf

# The unreleased working set is rewritten by hand and by tools/changelog-split
# on whatever platform the developer is on. Without this rule `core.autocrlf=true`
# makes every edit to CHANGELOG/unreleased/current.md show a phantom
# "LF will be replaced by CRLF" diff.
CHANGELOG/** text eol=lf
